(*
Upward-escaping closures (de-recursify deferred lift, Phase 1 — temp-vectors).

Two closures ESCAPE the method that defines them (returned via `^`, invoked
from `main` after that method's frame has popped):

  makeAdder: n  returns [:x | x + n]         — reads a captured ARG (n) after escape
  makeCounter   returns [ c:: c + 1. c ]     — reads AND mutates a captured TEMP (c)
                                               across repeated post-escape invocations

Under the old stack-home closure model the block's `home_ctx` is an absolute
pointer into the defining method's frame; once that frame is popped its
registers are reused by later calls, so reading/mutating the captured local
yields garbage (or crashes). This is correct only when the captured locals
live in a heap `Context` that outlives the defining frame — the temp-vector
lift this phase installs. The counter must read 1, 2, 3 (proving the SAME heap
slot persists and mutates across invocations), not 1, 1, 1 or garbage.

expect:
15
25
1
2
3
skip-berry: true
*)
class UpwardClosureTest packageUsing: manifest = () (
  makeAdder: n = ( ^ [ :x | x + n ] )
  makeCounter = ( | c | c:: 0. ^ [ c:: c + 1. c ] )
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | add5 ctr |
    add5:: makeAdder: 5.
    (add5 value: 10) println.
    (add5 value: 20) println.
    ctr:: makeCounter.
    (ctr value) println.
    (ctr value) println.
    (ctr value) println.
  )
) : (
)
